# SpatiumDDI Web UI bootstrap sentinel (#769).
#
# Keeps the Web UI (80/443) reachable from the very first boot, BEFORE
# the supervisor exists.
#
# Why this is needed. #285 Phase 6 made the supervisor-rendered
# spatium-role.nft authoritative for 80/443 and removed the accept from
# the base /etc/nftables.conf. But the supervisor can only render that
# drop-in after its first successful heartbeat — and on a control-plane
# appliance that heartbeat goes to the LOCAL api, so the port cannot open
# until the api is already serving. Measured on a clean install of
# dev-f3ca07f2-d796: boot 20:57:02, api Ready 20:59:39, drop-in applied
# 21:00:20 — the Web UI port opened 41 s AFTER the control plane was up.
#
# The practical effect was that an operator browsing a booting appliance
# got a ~3-minute connection timeout, and the "SpatiumDDI is initialising"
# page (#767 / #299) — which exists precisely for that window — was
# unreachable for the whole of it. Every probe from another host returned
# a filtered-port timeout; the first connection that succeeded already
# served the logged-in UI.
#
# The Web UI port is management surface, in the same category as :22,
# which the base config opens unconditionally. Role service ports (DNS /
# DHCP) correctly stay closed until a role is assigned; the Web UI should
# not.
#
# Retirement. The supervisor's spatium-role.nft carries the authoritative
# web-ui rule, and when the operator sets ``web_ui_allowed_cidrs`` (#285
# Phase 6) that rule is SOURCE-SCOPED. This file's unconditional accept
# sits earlier in the include glob (00- sorts first) and nftables accepts
# on first match, so leaving it in place would silently defeat that
# scoping. The renderers therefore stamp ``# spatium-webui: retire`` in
# the drop-in header whenever a scope is configured, and
# spatium-firewall-reload moves this file aside to ``.retired``. Clearing
# the scope stamps ``keep`` and restores it. Unscoped (the default), both
# rules say the same thing, so the sentinel simply stays.
#
# Same lifecycle as 00-spatium-k3s-bootstrap.nft: lives in /etc so it
# rides the /etc overlay onto /var and survives A/B slot swaps, and its
# ``.retired`` form persists the same way.
#
# This fragment is included INSIDE `chain input` by the
# `include "/etc/nftables.d/*.nft"` glob in /etc/nftables.conf, so it is
# a bare rule (no table/chain wrapper).
tcp dport { 80, 443 } accept comment "web-ui-bootstrap"
